ASPIRATIONAL — store scalar widening, TIER 1 REMAINDER (690_020): mixed scalar
columns in a CONTAINER (capacity > 1). NOT built yet.

On the settled capacity model (690_050): capacity > 1 = container (rows). Its
column arrays are hardcoded [CAP]i64; the row-value struct is i64-typed; the
write plumbing carries one value_type. A non-i64 container column (f64/i32/f32)
is therefore silently mis-emitted or walled.

Grounded i64-hardcoded sites to widen (per-field types via field_types[]):
  - column array  `{fname}: [{CAP}]i64`   → `[{CAP}]{field_types[i]}`
  - row-value struct  `{fname}: i64`       → `: {field_types[i]}`
  - single value_type → per-field types (per-field write emission)
  - colRead / insert-write / stored-write: thread the column's type, not i64.

Precedent it's scoping not architecture: std/kernel:shape (390_001) runs f64
fields through the same transform substrate. 690_021 proves value-shape f64.

i64 columns (incl. the [tree] synthesized `parent` self-FK) must stay i64 and
keep working alongside widened columns. Scalars need NO ownership — the
string/reference (take/discharge) tier is the NEXT rung.
